          "type": "turn",
          "question": "my dads left arm has gone all achy n hes gone grey and sweaty, hes 62, is that somethin to worry about",
          "answer": "**Call emergency services now (999 in the UK, 911 in the US, or your local number). Do not wait to see if it passes.**\n\nYour father's symptoms match the red-flag guidance for a possible heart attack. That guidance says discomfort spreading to one or both arms, with a cold sweat or light-headedness, should be treated as a possible heart attack until proven otherwise. Greyness with sweating adds to that concern. (Source: \"Chest pain that may indicate a heart attack,\" emergency medicine and cardiology guidance.)\n\nThe automatic risk check returned \"routine\" because its fixed table has no entry for arm ache with greyness and sweating alone. That result should not reassure you. The chest pain guidance above is the closer match, so please act on it.\n\n**While you wait for help:**\n- Keep him sitting or lying down and calm. Do not let him walk around or drive himself.\n- Do not give him anything to eat or drink unless the emergency operator advises it.\n- If he is conscious and not allergic to aspirin, the operator may tell you to give one. Follow their instructions.\n- If he becomes unresponsive or stops breathing normally, start CPR if you know how, and tell the operator.\n\n**Possibilities by body system:** A clinician would need to check the heart and blood vessels first, because the arm pain, sweating and grey colour point there. The same guidance also lists breathing and the nervous system as areas to check. Only a medical team can tell what is actually happening.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
